  "query_preview": "..Mid level ridging building into the Great Basin this\nevening will become the dominant weather feature through\nThanksgiving. With this ridge in place, warmer temperatures aloft\nand light winds will result likely allow valley inversions to\nbecome established through Thanksgiving, which may allow for\npatchy early morning stratus/fog. A weak weather system fighting\nthrough this ridge will be splitting as it moves inland, bringing\nlittle more than an increase in cloud cover Wednesday. Low \nelevation temperatures will remain near to perhaps slightly above \nclimo through Thanksgiving, while higher elevations see a warming \ntrend.\n\nLate Friday into Saturday, a more robust shortwave will crest the\nGreat Basin ridge, and guidance has been shifting toward a more\namplified solution digging this wave through the central Rockies,\nand pushing a cold front through the Forecast area. Roughly 60% of\nensemble solutions (heavily influenced by EC members) now spread \nprecipitation into northern Utah Friday night into early Saturday\nas this front pushes through the region. \n\nConfidence remains fairly high in a transition to a more\nprogressive and active pattern next week. Timing and track of\nindividual waves and the associated chance for precipitation\nremains uncertain as would be expected this far out, but ensemble\nfamilies are in good agreement developing negative height \nanomalies during the first half of next week.",
